    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d569">

                <pb facs="0639" n="627" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg627"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00118 European War 1939/1920</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 10948</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-04-22">April 22, 1943</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        30.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that
                        Aranha<anchor n="27" xml:id="d569-f1"/> told me today that the Brazilian
                    Government hopes that the Brazilian Military Mission now in North Africa (my
                    telegrams Nos. 906 of February 22, 5 p.m. and 1229 of March 12, 4 p.m.)<anchor
                        n="28" xml:id="d569-f2"/> will be allowed to remain there indefinitely. He
                    added that he hoped that a rumor that had reached him that they would be
                    permitted to stay only six weeks was untrue.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jefferson Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d569-f1" n="27" xml:id="d569-f1-content">
                <p>Oswaldo Aranha, Brazilian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
